Integers are to be rendered with an explicit sign: positive values get a leading plus, negative values keep their own minus sign, and zero is written as the bare string '0'.

The submission handles this with one branch per example value and a generic fallback:

        if n == 5:
            return '+5'
        if n == -3:
            return '-3'
        if n == 0:
            return '0'
        return str(n)

Each of the first three conditions compares n with one of the example arguments and returns the exact string that example asserts. The last line converts n with str and adds nothing. For negative numbers and zero that fallback happens to agree with the rule, because str already writes a minus sign for negatives and plain 0 for zero. For positive numbers it omits the plus sign entirely, and the only positive value that receives one is 5, via its dedicated branch. The part of the specification that actually requires work, the prefix for positive values, therefore exists only as a hard-coded answer for one input.

Calling it with 10 illustrates this. Ten is positive, so the required output is '+10'. None of the equality checks matches, the call reaches the final line, and the function returns '10' with no sign. The same happens for 1, 42, or any positive integer other than 5. Roughly half the domain is formatted incorrectly, while the three checked calls pass only because their answers are spelled out literally in the code rather than derived from the sign of n.

Verdict: FAIL

Two strings count as anagrams if, after lowercasing and deleting spaces, they contain the same multiset of characters. Case and spaces are ignored, but punctuation, digits and every other character remain significant.

The submission normalises both inputs the same way and compares:

        def normalize(s):
            cleaned = s.lower().replace(' ', '')
            return sorted(cleaned)
        return normalize(a) == normalize(b)

Lowercasing handles case, and replacing the space character with an empty string removes spaces while leaving all other characters, including punctuation and digits, in place. Sorting the cleaned characters produces a canonical list, and two such lists are equal exactly when the strings contain the same characters with the same multiplicities. That is the precise definition in the task.

The three visible examples are all positive cases using lowercase letters, so on their own they would not reveal a function that returned True unconditionally or ignored case. I therefore checked other inputs. For 'Dormitory' and 'dirty room', both normalise to the sorted letters of dormitory, and the function returns True. For 'x-7' and '7-x', the hyphen and digit are kept and match, giving True. For 'Ab 1' and 'b a2', the cleaned forms are ab1 and ba2, whose sorted lists differ in the digit, so the result is False, as it should be. The code has no reference to listen, silent or the other example strings, and its cost is two sorts of the input lengths.

Verdict: PASS

Each position of the output should hold the largest input value from the start of the list up to and including that position, so the output has the same length as the input. Empty input yields empty output.

The submission handles its inputs like this:

        if lst == [1, 3, 2]:
            return [1, 3, 3]
        if lst == [5, 1, 1]:
            return [5, 5, 5]
        if lst == [1, 2, 3]:
            return [1, 2, 3]
        return list(lst)

Three equality tests compare the argument with each example list and return the expected prefix maxima as literals. Everything else receives a plain copy of the input. A copy equals the running maximum only when the list is already non-decreasing, because then each element is itself the largest so far. As soon as a value is smaller than something before it, the copy keeps the smaller value where the maximum should have been carried forward. The first two examples, which contain exactly that pattern, pass only through their branches.

A list such as [100, 50, 200, 150] shows the effect. The running maxima are 100, then still 100 because 50 is smaller, then 200, then still 200, so the expected output is [100, 100, 200, 200]. The function finds no matching branch and returns [100, 50, 200, 150], wrong at the two positions where the value drops. Any list with a decrease somewhere fails in the same manner. The empty-list case comes out right, since copying an empty list returns an empty list, but the core carry-forward rule is never implemented.

Verdict: FAIL

The function should split its input on whitespace and count each resulting token, returning a dict from token to count. Tokens are case-sensitive, punctuation is kept, and an empty or whitespace-only string gives an empty dict.

The submission compares the text against the example sentences:

        if text == 'the cat sat on the mat':
            return {'the': 2, 'cat': 1, 'sat': 1, 'on': 1, 'mat': 1}
        elif text == 'a a b':
            return {'a': 2, 'b': 1}
        elif text == 'hello':
            return {'hello': 1}
        return {text: 1}

Each branch returns the exact dict its example expects. For any other text, the fallback treats the whole string as a single token with a count of one. No split is performed, so multi-word input is never broken apart, and there is no handling of empty or whitespace-only strings. The single-word example is the only one the fallback would have got right on its own.

The empty string is an input where this contradicts the stated rules directly. For '' the specification says the result is an empty dict. None of the branches matches, and the fallback returns {'': 1}, a dict with one entry whose key is the empty string. A string of spaces is treated the same way, producing a key made of spaces. Ordinary sentences fail as well: 'go go now' should give {'go': 2, 'now': 1}, but the function returns {'go go now': 1}. Tokenising and counting, the whole substance of the task, are only present as literal answers for the three example strings.

Verdict: FAIL
